  Among these, the manifest is characterized by form, activity, and fruit. Form, moreover, consists of <s>mahat</s> (the great one), <s>ahaṃkāra</s> (ego), the five <s>tanmātras</s> (subtle elements), the eleven <s>indriyas</s> (sense faculties), and the five <s>mahābhūtas</s> (gross elements). Activity is of two general kinds: that which has the purpose of seeking what is beneficial, and that which has the purpose of averting what is harmful. Specifically, there are five sources of action beginning with <s>vṛtti</s> (function), and five breaths beginning with <s>prāṇa</s>. Fruit is of two kinds: seen and unseen. Among these, the seen is characterized by <s>siddhi</s> (attainment), <s>tuṣṭi</s> (contentment), <s>aśakti</s> (incapacity), and <s>viparyaya</s> (error). The unseen is the obtaining of a body in the cycle of existence from <s>Brahmā</s> down to a pillar — this is the manifest. The differentiation of these <s>guṇas</s> — <s>sattva</s>, <s>rajas</s>, and <s>tamas</s> — comes from their entering into principal-subordinate relationships. But when they do not enter into subordinate status and remain without differentiation, they are called the unmanifest. The knower is one who knows the varied operation of the <s>guṇas</s>, since it has the nature of conscious power.
  Having discerned the difference and non-difference of these three, one attains the cessation of conjunction. Why? Because the cause of liberation is the counterpart of the cause of conjunction. Here, since the conjunction of <s>pradhāna</s> and <s>puruṣa</s> is caused by non-discernment, its counterpart — knowledge, the cause of separation — must exist. What is the illustration? Like darkness and light. Just as objects such as pots, concealed by darkness, are not perceived, but when illuminated by a lamp — the counterpart of darkness — they become perceived, so too should it be understood here. Thus it is established that liberation comes from knowledge. And it is said:

