  Therefore that [body] alone is the 'city.' And the scripture says thus: '<s>Prāṇa</s>, <s>apāna</s>, <s>samāna</s>, <s>udāna</s>, and <s>vyāna</s> are the five breaths. The sixth is the mind. The seventh is the city [body], the eighth is speech.' By the mention of speech, the group of <s>karmendriyas</s> (action faculties) is indicated. By the mention of mind, the group of <s>buddhīndriyas</s> (cognitive sense faculties) is indicated. This eightfold [group] with <s>prāṇa</s>, being <s>vaikārika</s> (sattva-derived), is always the pillar-like constituent limb of the <s>guṇa</s>-body for the onlooking <s>kṣetrajña</s> (knower of the field) who assumes a body — it is uncuttable, unbreakable, unburnable, indestructible, and unshakeable. But the external, material [parts] — which are like grass and clay — are impermanent and subject to growth.
  [Objection:] From what, then, does this activity of <s>prāṇa</s> and the rest proceed? [Reply:] It proceeds from the <s>karmayonis</s> (sources of action). For <s>rajas</s>, having fallen from <s>mahat</s> and been modified, becomes the five <s>karmayonis</s>, which are like eggs: <s>dhṛti</s> (steadfastness), <s>śraddhā</s> (faith), <s>sukhā</s> (desire for happiness), <s>vividiṣā</s> (desire to know), and <s>avividiṣā</s> (absence of desire to know). And it is said:

